{"name":"Acacia","hex":"#dbcd64","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Acacia","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A taxonomic genus within the family Leguminosae – mostly thornless Australasian shrubs and trees, called acacias or wattles. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A genus that included all the genera now included in tribe Acacieae."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A barangay of Catarman, Northern Samar, Philippines."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Acacia"}],"entries":[{"title":"Acacia","blocks":[{"text":"Acacia, commonly known as wattles or acacias, is a genus of about 1,084 species of shrubs and trees in the subfamily Mimosoideae of the pea family Fabaceae. Initially, it comprised a group of plant species native to Africa, South America, and Australasia, but is now reserved for species mainly from Australia, with others from New Guinea, Southeast Asia, and the Indian Ocean."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Acacia_baileyana_3%2C_Canberra_ACT.JPG/330px-Acacia_baileyana_3%2C_Canberra_ACT.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acacia","wikidata":"Q81666"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Acacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg/500px-Acacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Acacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg/1280px-Acacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Acacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg/500px-Acacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bipinnate leaves of Acacia dealbata","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAcacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.